

CRYPT(3)            UNIX Programmer's Manual            CRYPT(3)



NAME
     crypt - hash a password

SYNOPSIS
     char *crypt(key, salt)
     char *key, *salt;

     char *crypt_salt(hash)
     char *hash;

DESCRIPTION
     Crypt hashes key against salt and returns a pointer to a
     static buffer holding the result in the tagged form

          $f$xy$hhhhhhhh

     where xy is the two-character salt, verbatim, and hhhhhhhh
     is eight hex digits.  The buffer is overwritten by the next
     call; a caller that needs to keep the result copies it out.

     login(1), su(1) and passwd(1) are the current callers.  All
     dispatch on the leading "$f$" tag to recognise a hash of
     this form, never on the string's length, so a second hash
     format can be added later without breaking stored entries.

     _C_r_y_p_t___s_a_l_t returns the salt that produced a stored hash: the
     two characters after the tag for a tagged hash, and the
     hash itself for an untagged one, which is its own salt.
     It is the function that dispatch is done WITH.

     CHECKING A PASSWORD IS

          strcmp(stored, crypt(given, crypt_salt(stored)))

     and never _c_r_y_p_t(given, stored).  The second form is
     upstream's and is right for DES, where a hash is its own
     salt; against a tagged hash it folds "$f" where the salt
     belongs and returns "$f$$f$........", which equals no
     stored hash at all -- so every password is rejected,
     including the correct one.  _s_u(1) did that and could
     not authenticate anybody (apple/fgx#414).

ALGORITHM
     THIS IS DELIBERATELY NOT DES CRYPT.  FGX's crypt is FNV-1a,
     a cheap 32-bit non-cryptographic hash, folded over salt then
     key and printed as eight hex digits.  A 25-round DES crypt at
     2.8 MHz would make every login visibly pause, and buys little
     on a single-user IIgs with no shared password database to
     defend across a network.

     The "$f$" tag is a forward-compatibility seam, not a version
     stamp that happens to be used once: a real DES crypt can be
     added later and coexist in the same /etc/passwd, because a
     13-character untagged DES hash cannot be confused with a
     tagged FNV-1a one.  Any code that reads hashes must dispatch
     on the tag, not on length.

SECURITY
     THE THREAT MODEL IS DELIBERATELY LIMITED.  This hash stops a
     shoulder-surfer reading the screen and a casual reader of
     /etc/passwd -- the password does not appear in the clear, and
     two accounts that choose the same password do not get the
     same hash provided their salts differ.

     It is NOT a defence against an attacker who already has the
     file and wants the password.  FNV-1a is a 32-bit
     non-cryptographic hash with no per-call cost; it was designed
     for hash tables, not for resisting an offline guesser, and it
     is brute-forced in a fraction of a second on any host built
     in this decade.  Treat a copy of /etc/passwd as equivalent to
     the plaintext passwords it hashes.

RETURN VALUE
     A pointer to a static 14-byte buffer, valid until the next
     call to crypt().  There is no failure return; a null or empty
     salt or key is treated as absent, not an error.

FILES
     /etc/passwd            stores the hashed form

SEE ALSO
     login(1), passwd(1), getpass(3)

BUGS
     There is no per-installation pepper and no iteration count:
     the cost of computing one hash is fixed and small by design,
     which is also exactly its weakness against an offline
     attacker (see SECURITY).



